      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in Phoenix often stems from a complex interaction of environmental stresses. High diurnal temperature swings induce significant thermal expansion and contraction in the asphalt binder, leading to tensile stresses. If the bitumen's viscosity is too low, it may lack the necessary stiffness to resist these stresses, while poor subgrade stability allows for underlying support loss, concentrating strain and initiating cracks. Addressing material aging and optimizing binder selection are key to mitigating this thermal-mechanical failure pathway.",
