    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Could you go a bit deeper into the subgrade aspect? We're worried that maybe the compaction wasn't uniform across the entire stretch and that's causing localized settling. How would inconsistent subgrade compaction specifically interact with those thermal stresses you mentioned to accelerate the longitudinal cracking?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inconsistent subgrade compaction creates localized areas of reduced bearing capacity and increased settlement potential under load. When the asphalt encounters differential settlement, it forces localized strain concentrations, effectively pre-stressing the pavement structure. These stress hot spots then intersect with the tensile stresses generated by thermal cycling, causing the pavement to crack prematurely along these weak lines rather than uniform thermal expansion fractures. Therefore, poor compaction transforms predictable thermal stress into localized, accelerating structural failure.",
      "source": "target",
